## Initial Q&A
### On what date did Nigeria officially gain independence from British colonial rule?
Answer: Nigeria officially gained independence from British colonial rule on October 1, 1960.

Source URL: https://www.aljazeera.com/news/2020/9/30/nigeria-turns-60-hope-despite-anger-over-corruption-poverty

### What was the most recent census conducted in Nigeria before its independence on October 1, 1960?
Answer: The most recent census conducted in Nigeria before its independence on October 1, 1960, was in 1952. As noted in the source, "the last census in 1952" is referenced as the benchmark for population figures prior to the first post-independence census in 1962.

Source URL: https://qz.com/africa/1221472/the-story-of-how-nigerias-census-figures-became-weaponized

### What population figure was recorded in the Nigerian census conducted closest to independence in 1960?
Answer: The census conducted closest to Nigerian independence was in 1962–1963. According to the source, "A new census was held in 1963 and this time, an additional 8.5 million people were discovered in the north bringing the total to 31 million for the north...and 56 million for Nigeria as a whole." Therefore, the population figure recorded in the Nigerian census closest to independence was **56 million**, which is notably higher than the 45 million figure cited in Buhari's claim.

Source URL: https://qz.com/africa/1221472/the-story-of-how-nigerias-census-figures-became-weaponized

### Was the 1952–1953 Nigerian census considered accurate and reliable by demographers and historians at the time?
Answer: No, the 1952–1953 Nigerian census was not considered accurate and reliable, as demographers and historians note that accusations of rigged figures date back to the 1950s, when "the British authorities were accused of skewing census figures to favour the interests of northern political elite."

Source URL: https://africacheck.org/fact-checks/factsheets/factsheet-nigerias-population-figures

